# 4. DATA MODEL: DESIGN THIS CAREFULLY

This is one of the most important parts of the implementation.

The current assessment system stores answerable questions in a flat `questions[]` array.

The existing marking, grading, result calculations, manual grading and question snapshots depend on this structure.

**Do not simply insert comprehension passages or generic text blocks into the existing questions array and treat them as questions.**

That would risk breaking:

- Question validation.
- Mark calculations.
- Total assessment scores.
- Automated grading.
- Manual grading.
- Required-question validation.
- Response counts.
- Result reports.
- Question navigation.
- Existing student attempts.

Instead, introduce a separate, versioned assessment-layout structure.

A suggested conceptual model is:

```ts
assessmentLayout: {
  version: 1,
  sections: [
    {
      id: "section_1",
      title: "Section A",
      description: "Comprehension",
      items: [
        {
          id: "content_1",
          kind: "content",
          contentType: "instruction",
          title: "Instructions",
          body: "Read the passage carefully."
        },
        {
          id: "comprehension_1",
          kind: "comprehension",
          passage: {
            id: "passage_1",
            title: "The Village by the River",
            body: "Full passage..."
          },
          questionIds: [
            "question_1",
            "question_2",
            "question_3"
          ]
        },
        {
          id: "question_item_1",
          kind: "question",
          questionId: "question_4"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

This is a conceptual starting point. Adapt it to the existing codebase and its conventions.

The critical rules are:

1. Actual answerable questions remain in `questions[]`.
2. Content blocks are stored separately in `assessmentLayout`.
3. Comprehension groups reference existing question IDs.
4. Every answerable question should appear exactly once in the assessment layout.
5. A question must not accidentally become part of multiple comprehension groups.
6. Every content block and question must have stable IDs.
7. Reordering the assessment should not regenerate IDs.
8. Content blocks should never contribute marks.
9. Layout data must be validated and safely persisted by the backend.

Update the required TypeScript interfaces and declarations.

Pay special attention to `normalizeQuestions()` and `validateExamCreationPayload()` in `api/_lib/jdi-assessment.js`.

The current normalization logic explicitly constructs normalized question objects. Additional fields can be discarded unless they are deliberately supported.

You must ensure that:

- Existing question validation remains intact.
- Layout data is independently validated.
- Invalid question references are rejected.
- Duplicate layout references are prevented.
- Passage content has reasonable size limits.
- Unsupported block types are rejected.
- Missing layout data is handled safely.

Update the tutor-safe and student-safe projections as appropriate.

Relevant locations include `tutorSafe()` in `netlify/functions/jdi-examinations.ts` and the response construction in `netlify/functions/examination-attempt.ts`.

Do not expose correct answers, accepted answers, marking explanations or other restricted information in student-facing content projections.

