# 18. PERFORMANCE, FIRESTORE AND RELIABILITY

Our priorities are balance, stability, low resource consumption and predictable behaviour.

Do not introduce unnecessary Firestore reads or writes.

In particular:

- Do not fetch every question or content block independently.
- Do not create a separate Firestore document for every passage unless the existing architecture genuinely requires it.
- Prefer storing the validated assessment layout with its parent assessment.
- Capture the attempt layout snapshot when the attempt is created.
- Reuse existing assessment-fetching logic where appropriate.
- Avoid unnecessary listeners.
- Avoid excessive React re-renders when typing long passages.
- Do not introduce frequent writes for every individual keystroke.
- Preserve existing autosave and debounce patterns.
- Avoid expensive client-side reconstruction on every render.

Use stable React keys and memoization where beneficial.

Do not introduce unnecessary new npm packages.

Any new persistence logic must follow the existing database conventions and security boundaries.

